===Matchmaking===
Players are matched based on their current Elo. The higher the Elo of your opponents, the more Elo you earn. Players can queue solo, in a team of 2, or in a team of 3, except in {{Color|0c0|Masters I}} and above where players can only queue solo.

===Floor Protection===
*Once a player reaches a new Rank, they can get demoted to a lower Rank.
*In {{Color|69f|Silver}}, players can never demote to Bronze because Rank reset only resets until Silver I.
*In {{Color|fc0|Gold}} and {{Color|3cf|Diamond}}, players cannot demote below the full Rank for the duration of the season. However, it is still possible to be demoted within a Rank. For example, losing matches in Gold II can demote back to Gold I; there is no floor protection in this case.
*In {{Color|93f|Mythic}}, {{Color|f30|Legendary}}, and {{Color|0c0|Masters}}, players can demote below the full Rank if their Elo is excactly at the threshold. For example, players cannot drop from Mythic I to Diamond III if they have more than 4500 Elo, unless a game is lost at 4500 Elo.
*In {{Color|666|Pro}}, there is no floor protection; players can drop to Masters III even if they have more than 11250 Elo.

===Elo System===
The amount of Elo gained or lost after a match depends on your current Rank and the outcome of the match (see table below), but it can be influenced by the Elo difference of the opposing team. There is a minimum amount of Elo that can be gained and lost for each Rank.

When playing solo, the amount of Elo earned depends on your team's average Elo in comparison to the opposing team's average Elo. When playing in a team, the amount of Elo earned depends on your own Elo in comparison to the opposing team's average Elo. If your own or your team's average Elo is higher than the opposing team's, you will gain less Elo for a win and lose more for a loss. If it is lower, you will gain more Elo for a win and lose less for a loss.

The formula below describes how much additional Elo is gained or lost at the end of a match, where E is the amount of additional Elo earned, and R is your Elo advantage over the opponent. Afterwards, the result is rounded to the nearest whole number.

:<math>E=200(1+10^{R/2000})^{-1}-100</math>

'''Epic win!''' / '''Close match!''': If the game ends in a 2-0 victory or 1-2 defeat, you gain a certain percentage more or lose less. The exact amount depends on the Rank; the lower the Rank, the higher the percentage.

If a boost from the previous season is active and the result is a net posivite, the result is multiplied by 1.1x, always being rounded down. A Ranked boost does not make you lose less Elo on defeats, despite the visual appearance.

